<title>USA: US House rejects cut in crop insurance commissions.</title>
<headline>US House rejects cut in crop insurance commissions.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-07-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. House defeated 195-230 a proposal to remove $36 million earmarked for crop insurance commissions and put it into the Women, Infants and Children feeding program.</p>
<p>The fiscal 1998 agriculture appropriations bill contained $188.6 million for the sales commissions, $36 million more than the White House requested.</p>
<p>Proponents said the sales commission rate could be cut slightly without causing any harm because more money had been alloted than was requested.</p>
<p>&quot;Farmers are not hurt by this amendment,&quot; said Representative David Obey.</p>
<p>Farm-state lawmakers said a lower commission rates would make it harder for farmers to obtain crop insurance to offset the risk of a crop failure.</p>
</text>
